{"ts":"2026-09-09T06:19:34.46302559Z","session":"00f1ab56-8e0a-4c22-8f2c-ed8da8891ca9","tool":"Agent","tool_input":{"description":"Research Tauri 2 testing/toolchain","name":"research-tauri","prompt":"Research task: I'm planning a Tauri 2.x desktop app (developer tool, local-first, fronts a Go CLI/daemon) and need the CURRENT (September 2026) state of the Tauri ecosystem with a huge focus on automated testing. Verify against current docs/web, don't rely on training data.\n\nQuestions to answer, with sources:\n1. Current stable Tauri version and notable 2.x capabilities relevant to a dev tool: sidecar/shell plugin (bundling an external Go binary), filesystem watching, updater, deep links, multi-window, security/capabilities model.\n2. The testing story, precisely:\n   - WebDriver E2E: tauri-driver — which platforms are supported today? (Historically Linux + Windows only, NO macOS — confirm current status.) WebdriverIO vs Selenium integration maturity.\n   - Frontend unit/component testing with mocked IPC: @tauri-apps/api/mocks (mockIPC/mockWindows) — current API and limitations.\n   - Rust-side testing: tauri::test mock runtime maturity.\n   - What real projects do in CI (GitHub Actions) for Tauri apps: common pyramid shapes, e.g. Playwright against the vite dev server (browser, no Tauri) vs true tauri-driver runs.\n3. Frontend stack consensus for Tauri dev tools in 2026: React vs Svelte vs SolidJS prevalence, Vite, Tailwind v4 status, component libraries commonly paired (shadcn/ui etc.), TanStack Query/Router fit.\n4. Toolchain and quality gates: recommended lints (typescript-eslint strict, clippy), formatting, type-checking in CI, cargo-deny/audit, bundle size and startup-time expectations, cross-platform CI build matrix costs (ubuntu/macos runners), code signing/notarization pain for macOS.\n5. Known Tauri pain points for this app class (webview inconsistencies across platforms — WebKitGTK on Linux vs WKWebView on macOS, IPC payload size limits for large JSONL logs, virtualized list rendering of huge logs).\n\nOutput: a compact structured report (markdown, under ~180 lines) organized by the five questions, each answer with a confidence marker (verified-current vs plausible-training-data) and links. Do not write any files; return the report as your final message.","subagent_type":"quarry:researcher"},"tool_input_hash":"5bf017c29919861be0831dc57962e894dd49c60dd71d247b61eb07c3a00e412f","tool_input_preview":"{\"description\":\"Research Tauri 2 testing/toolchain\",\"name\":\"research-tauri\",\"prompt\":\"Research task: I'm planning a Tauri 2.x desktop app (developer tool, local-first, fronts a Go CLI/daemon) and need..."}
