{"ts":"2026-09-09T06:21:20.172475107Z","session":"00f1ab56-8e0a-4c22-8f2c-ed8da8891ca9","agent_id":"aresearch-block-8b4f468c95430565","agent_type":"research-block","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"# Block \"buzz\" research report\n\n## 1. What is \"buzz…","message":"# Block \"buzz\" research report\n\n## 1. What is \"buzz\"?\n\n`github.com/block/buzz` is real, from 2026 — but it is **not** a desktop app. It's a self-hostable team+agent collaboration workspace built on **Nostr** (keypair identity, cryptographically signed events). Core ideas: every actor (human or agent) has its own signing keypair; an owner issues a narrowly-scoped authorization to an agent, which then signs its own commits/messages with its own identity; Git repos are stored as immutable content-addressed packfiles + a mutable manifest pointer on object storage (atomic compare-and-swap, no Git server); a \"forge\" UI shows PRs/branches/activity; designed for one frontier agent driving a swarm of cheaper agents in shared channels. No Tauri/Electron mentioned, no client architecture detail in the launch post. ([engineering.block.xyz/blog/buzz](https://engineering.block.xyz/blog/buzz))\n\n**Verdict: the founder almost certainly means Berd, not Buzz** — or is conflating the two Block projects. Berd is Block's actual Tauri-based desktop app, publicly launched days ago (Aug 18-19, 2026), and its architecture is exactly the \"Tauri desktop fronting a local backend\" pattern that fits ethos. Buzz solves a different problem (multi-agent org coordination over Nostr/Git), not desktop UI shape.\n\n## 2. Goose desktop architecture (the backend Berd/goose-desktop both use)\n\n- **`goosed`**: a Rust daemon (the `goose-server` crate, built on **axum**) exposing goose's agent loop over **REST + WebSocket** (moving to **ACP** — Agent Client Protocol — as the unifying transport across CLI/desktop/IDE/TUI clients). Handles session lifecycle, config, TLS, secret-key auth, remote tunnels.\n- **Repo layout**: one Cargo workspace with `goose` (core lib), `goose-cli`, `goose-server` (→ `goosed` binary), `goose-mcp` (MCP extension servers), `goose-acp`. Desktop app lives in its own subtree, historically Electron + React, now migrating to Tauri.\n- **Electron → Tauri v2 migration** (in flight, tracked in [aaif-goose/goose#7332](https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose/discussions/7332)): Rust backend (~1,600 LOC / 8 modules — `commands.rs` 42 IPC handlers, `goosed.rs` sidecar lifecycle, `tray.rs`, `menu.rs`, `settings.rs`, `wakelock.rs`, `dock.rs`) replaces a 2,394-line Electron `main.ts`. React 19 + Vite frontend kept almost unchanged via a `tauri-bridge.ts` shim providing `window.electron` compatibility — 40+ frontend files needed zero changes. 17 Tauri plugins used instead of hand-rolled Node equivalents (dialog, fs, shell, notification, global-shortcut, deep-link, **updater**, window-state, etc.). Reported gains: ~150MB bundle-size savings (system webview vs bundled Chromium), lower idle memory, native IPC/process-management performance.\n\n## 3. Berd — the actual reference to copy\n\n`github.com/block/berd` (Apache 2.0, Tauri 2 + React 19). This is the closest analog to what you want to build for ethos:\n\n- **Sidecar pattern**: Berd bundles `goose serve` as a child process; talks to it over the **ACP WebSocket**. Clean separation — goose owns the agent loop, Berd owns projects/sessions/context/agent config/UI shell. Swap \"goose serve\" for \"ethos serve\" (already exists, stdio MCP today) and this maps almost directly.\n- **Local-first data model**: conversation/session history in a local SQLite-ish session DB; **credentials in the OS keychain**, not config files. (Ethos analog: mission/session YAML+JSONL already local-first in-repo; secrets — if any — belong in OS keychain, not `.punt-labs/`.)\n- **Distribution seams**: the public repo has explicit extension points for enterprise/managed distributions (managed provider settings, private resources, release infra) without forking — open-core style, à la Terraform.\n- **Governance note**: Berd itself takes no external PRs (look-and-use, not community-owned) — a governance choice, not an architecture one; irrelevant to copying the pattern.\n\n## 4. Patterns to copy / avoid for ethos's Tauri app\n\n**Copy:**\n- Tauri 2 + a plain web frontend (React or similar) talking to your **existing Go binary as a sidecar**, not a rewrite. `ethos serve` already speaks MCP over stdio — either keep stdio and shell out per-call, or add a local WebSocket/HTTP mode (axum-style) if the UI needs push updates (mission progress, audit events streaming).\n- One protocol, one server, many clients — goose's ACP lesson: don't let CLI and desktop drift onto separate integration paths (goose's own postmortem: \"every new feature had to be wired up in both places\" under the old Electron+REST split). For ethos, keep the CLI and the Tauri app both going through the same `ethos serve` MCP surface.\n- OS keychain for anything credential-shaped; local files (already the case) for everything else.\n- Ship Tauri's `updater` plugin for auto-update from day one rather than bolting it on later.\n- Leave enterprise/private extension seams in the public repo layout if this is ever going to have a paid/managed variant — cheap to design in now, expensive to retrofit.\n\n**Avoid:**\n- Don't build a REST+SSE bespoke server as an intermediate step the way goose originally did with `goosed` v1 — go straight to a single well-defined transport (even a simple JSON-RPC-over-stdio-or-websocket) so you don't inherit goose's dual-maintenance problem.\n- Don't reach for Electron \"because it's more familiar\" — the size/memory case for Tauri (system webview vs bundled Chromium, ~150MB+ and large idle-RAM savings) is now well-documented across both goose's own migration and general 2025-2026 Tauri-vs-Electron benchmarks.\n- Don't let the desktop app own business logic that belongs in the Go binary — Berd's \"front end is replaceable, backend owns the loop\" split is the right layering; ethos's mission/identity/audit logic must stay in the Go binary, with Tauri as a thin shell.\n\n## Links\n- [Buzz launch post](https://engineering.block.xyz/blog/buzz) / [github.com/block/buzz](https://github.com/block/buzz)\n- [github.com/block/berd](https://github.com/block/berd)\n- [Berd coverage — VentureBeat](https://venturebeat.com/orchestration/blocks-new-apache-2-0-agent-workspace-berd-works-across-models-and-harnesses-stores-conversation-history-locally)\n- [Berd coverage — Dataconomy](https://dataconomy.com/2026/08/19/block-open-sources-berd-ai-agent-desktop-app/)\n- [goose Electron→Tauri migration discussion](https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose/discussions/7332)\n- [goose 2.0 / ACP blog post](https://goose-docs.ai/blog/2026/04/08/goose-acp-and-new-tui/)\n- [DeepWiki: goose-server / API layer](https://deepwiki.com/aaif-goose/goose/5-server-and-api-layer-(goose-server))\n\nBottom line: \"buzz\" as literally described doesn't exist as a desktop-app reference — it's a Nostr-based team/agent coordination platform. **Berd is the model to actually copy.** Worth flagging to the founder before scoping the design doc, since the two projects solve very different problems and \"modelled off buzz\" vs \"modelled off Berd\" would point the design in different directions.","recipient":"team-lead","summary":"Buzz vs Berd research — report ready","to":"team-lead","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"8f470add6777577cab301cfbc8b58790b36e3f25f9c816012608250d877021e1","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"# Block \\\"buzz\\\" research report\\n\\n## 1. What is \\\"buzz…\",\"message\":\"# Block \\\"buzz\\\" research report\\n\\n## 1. What is \\\"buzz\\\"?\\n\\n`github.com/block/buzz` is real, from 2026 — but it..."}
