{"ts":"2026-09-09T06:22:48.392380302Z","session":"00f1ab56-8e0a-4c22-8f2c-ed8da8891ca9","agent_id":"aresearch-tauri-ca15bba12b783a3b","agent_type":"research-tauri","tool":"SendMessage","tool_input":{"content":"# Tauri 2.x Ecosystem \u0026 Testing Research (Sept 20…","message":"# Tauri 2.x Ecosystem \u0026 Testing Research (Sept 2026)\n\n## 1. Version \u0026 capabilities relevant to a dev tool [verified-current]\n\n- **Stable line**: v2.10.1 (Mar 4, 2026) → core crate 2.11.5 by Jul 2026. v2 GA'd Oct 2, 2024. ([Tauri releases](https://tauri.app/release/core/), [v2.0 blog](https://v2.tauri.app/blog/tauri-20/))\n- **Sidecar/shell**: no separate \"sidecar plugin\" — bundling an external Go binary is done via the **shell plugin** (`tauri-plugin-shell`, v2.3.5 Feb 2026) using its `Command::sidecar()` API + `externalBin` config. Standard pattern for wrapping a Go CLI/daemon.\n- **Filesystem watching**: the old standalone `tauri-plugin-fs-watch` is now a read-only mirror; watching was folded into unified `tauri-plugin-fs` (v2.5.1, May 2026), using `notify` v8 + `notify-debouncer-full` v0.6, exposing both immediate and debounced JS watch APIs. Cross-platform caveat: inotify (Linux) vs FSEvents (macOS) vs ReadDirectoryChangesW (Windows) behave differently under rapid-fire changes. ([fs plugin docs.rs](https://docs.rs/crate/tauri-plugin-fs/latest), [v2 fs reference](https://v2.tauri.app/reference/javascript/fs/))\n- **Updater**: `tauri-plugin-updater` at 2.10.1 (Apr 2026); ships full-binary updates (no diffing — bundles are small enough it doesn't matter); recently added `allowDowngrades`.\n- **Deep links**: `tauri-plugin-deep-link` 2.4.9 (May 2026); macOS universal links now configurable via `plugins.deep-link.desktop.domains`.\n- **Multi-window / security model**: Tauri v2's capabilities model is deny-by-default (a real change from v1's implicit-allow). Capabilities are JSON/TOML files under `src-tauri/capabilities/`, scoped by **window label** (not title) and optionally by `webviews` glob for multi-webview windows. Best practice: one capability file per concern (fs.json, dialog.json, etc.), least-privilege per window, and treat window-creation APIs as high-privilege-only (a lower-priv window that can spawn windows is a privilege-escalation vector). ([Capabilities docs](https://v2.tauri.app/security/capabilities/), [multi-window guide](https://v2.tauri.app/learn/security/capabilities-for-windows-and-platforms/))\n\n## 2. Testing story [verified-current, Sept 2026 snapshot]\n\n**WebDriver E2E (tauri-driver)** — confirmed still Linux + Windows only, no macOS:\n- Native `tauri-driver` has never gained macOS support ([tauri-apps/tauri#7068](https://github.com/tauri-apps/tauri/issues/7068), open since 2023, still unresolved). Reason: Apple provides no WebDriver implementation for WKWebView (unlike WebKitWebDriver on Linux, Edge WebDriver on Windows).\n- **The gap now has workarounds** (this is the part that's changed since your \"historically\" framing): WebdriverIO's `@wdio/tauri-service` now supports macOS via an *embedded* WebDriver server inside the app itself (via `tauri-plugin-wdio-webdriver`, free/OSS — no external driver process needed on any platform), or via CrabNebula's cross-platform `tauri-driver` fork (paid API key required for macOS). A third-party OSS project, `tauri-webdriver` (Choochmeque), also claims native WKWebView/WebView2/WebKitGTK control on all three platforms. None of these are first-party Tauri. ([WebdriverIO platform support](https://webdriver.io/docs/desktop-testing/tauri/platform-support/), [tauri-webdriver](https://github.com/Choochmeque/tauri-webdriver))\n- WebdriverIO integration is the maturest path today; Selenium is still usable (official docs show it) but WebdriverIO has the active desktop-testing investment (platform-support docs, troubleshooting guide, embedded driver).\n\n**Frontend unit/component testing with mocked IPC** — `@tauri-apps/api/mocks` [verified-current]:\n- `mockWindows(...)` fakes window *existence* only (labels), not properties.\n- `mockIPC(handler, { shouldMockEvents: true })` intercepts `invoke()` and can simulate `listen`/`emit` (but not `emitTo`).\n- `clearMocks()` must be called between tests (e.g. `afterEach`) if your test runner reuses the `window` object.\n- v1→v2 breaking change: detection moved from `window.__TAURI_METADATA__` to `window.__TAURI_INTERNALS__.metadata`. ([Mocking guide](https://v2.tauri.app/develop/tests/mocking/), [API reference](https://v2.tauri.app/reference/javascript/api/namespacemocks/))\n\n**Rust-side `tauri::test` mock runtime** [verified-current, explicitly marked unstable by Tauri]:\n- `tauri::test::mock_builder()` + `MockRuntime` lets you build a real `App`/`WebviewWindow` without an OS webview, then drive it via `get_ipc_response()` for full invoke-path tests, or call command functions directly against `mock_app()` for pure logic + managed-`State` tests.\n- Docs.rs explicitly flags the module unstable — API has shifted across versions before (e.g., a 1.6.7→1.6.8 regression). Treat as adequate for command/state logic, not a guaranteed-stable long-term contract.\n- Third-party `tauri-test` crate exists for JSDOM-bridged full-stack integration tests (invoke real Rust from Vitest) — newer/less proven than official `tauri::test`. ([tauri::test docs](https://docs.rs/tauri/latest/tauri/test/index.html))\n\n**What real projects do in CI** [verified-current via example repos + official docs]:\n- Two pyramid shapes coexist, no single consensus:\n  1. **Official tauri-driver + WebdriverIO**, run on Linux under a fake X display (Xvfb) + Windows GH-hosted runners — true native-webview E2E, but no macOS lane without the paid/embedded workarounds above. Official CI guide walks through this. ([CI guide](https://v2.tauri.app/develop/tests/webdriver/ci/))\n  2. **Playwright against the Vite dev server in browser mode** (no Tauri binary at all) — official docs describe this explicitly: plain Chrome hits the dev server, `invoke()` calls are intercepted/mocked via the same WDIO-style API. Faster, fully cross-platform in CI, but doesn't touch the real WebView, sidecar process, native menus, or window management. `srsholmes/tauri-playwright` is a concrete OSS example doing exactly this, and calls out that real CDP-based Playwright only works on Windows via WebView2 — macOS/Linux WebViews aren't CDP-drivable at all.\n- **Net pyramid for a dev tool fronting a Go daemon**: heavy Rust unit tests (`tauri::test`) + heavy frontend unit/component tests (Vitest + `mockIPC`) as the base, a thin Playwright-against-dev-server layer for UI logic, and a *thin* true tauri-driver/WebdriverIO smoke layer on Linux+Windows only (accept macOS E2E gap or pay for CrabNebula/embedded-driver).\n\n## 3. Frontend stack consensus [verified-current, but \"consensus\" is soft — synthesized from multiple 2026 sources, not a single authority]\n\n- **React + Vite remains the dominant pairing** for Tauri apps; officially templated via `create-tauri-app` (also Vue, Svelte, SolidJS, Angular, vanilla, Yew/Leptos/Sycamore for Rust-frontend). Vite is Tauri's recommended bundler for all SPA frameworks.\n- **Svelte/SvelteKit** is the credible smallest-bundle alternative, officially templated, straightforward `invoke()` integration.\n- **SolidJS** is officially templated but has a noticeably thinner UI-library ecosystem — no strong shadcn-equivalent.\n- **Tailwind v4** is current and paired with **shadcn/ui**, which as of **July 2026 switched its default primitive layer from Radix UI to Base UI** (existing Radix-based projects need no migration; new `shadcn` installs default to Base UI). shadcn/ui remains React-only; Svelte/Vue ports lag. \"Tauri UI\" (`create-tauri-ui`) is a maintained shadcn+Tauri scaffold (React/Next/SvelteKit) producing ~2–2.5MB bundles.\n- **TanStack**: no Tauri-specific guidance found, but TanStack Query/Router are framework-idiomatic fits for any Vite+React Tauri app and commonly paired in starter templates (not verified against a Tauri-specific authority — plausible/standard web-dev consensus, not confirmed via a Tauri source).\n\n## 4. Toolchain \u0026 quality gates\n\n- **Lints/format**: typical CI sequence — `eslint` + `prettier --check` + `tsc --noEmit` (frontend), `cargo fmt --check` + `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -D warnings` + `cargo test` (Rust). Multi-crate workspaces should run clippy/tests per crate, especially where features are mutually exclusive. [verified-current example: dannysmith/tauri-template]\n- **cargo-audit vs cargo-deny**: complementary, not redundant — `cargo-audit` checks published RustSec advisories against `Cargo.lock` (must be committed for binary/app crates like Tauri); `cargo-deny` additionally does license-policy enforcement, crate-source restriction, and duplicate-dependency detection. Common pattern: run both as separate CI gates.\n- **Bundle size / startup expectations** [verified-current, but figures come from vendor/benchmark-aggregator blogs, not Tauri's own docs — treat multipliers as directional]: Tauri apps commonly land 2–10MB vs Electron's 80–200MB (~20–50x); startup roughly ~4x faster; memory 58–75% lower. All sources caveat this is best-case / depends heavily on code quality and app complexity — one N=1 hands-on benchmark explicitly flags itself as not rigorous.\n- **Cross-platform CI cost** [verified-current pricing, Jan 2026 GitHub repricing]: Linux runner $0.006/min, Windows $0.010/min, **macOS $0.062/min** — and macOS consumes plan-included minutes at a **10x multiplier** against Linux-equivalent minutes (so 2,000 free minutes ≈ only 200 min of macOS CI). This makes a full 3-OS build+test matrix on every push meaningfully expensive; teams commonly restrict macOS/Windows lanes to release builds or nightly rather than every PR.\n- **macOS code signing/notarization** [verified-current, multiple 2026 sources]: still the single biggest CI/distribution pain point, largely orthogonal to Tauri itself. Requires a paid Apple Developer account (~$99/yr — notarization is unavailable on the free tier). CI needs `APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY`, `APPLE_CERTIFICATE` (base64 .p12), `APPLE_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD`, `APPLE_ID`/`APPLE_PASSWORD` (app-specific password) or the newer App Store Connect API-key flow (`API_KEY_ID`/`API_KEY_ISSUER_ID`/`API_KEY`, avoids Gatekeeper bypass workarounds). Notarization normally takes 1–5 min; anything stuck past 20 min is hung, not slow. There's an open Tauri issue specifically about notarization failures when using `externalBin`/sidecars on macOS ([#11992](https://github.com/tauri-apps/tauri/issues/11992)) — directly relevant since this app sidecars a Go binary. Common advice: consider releasing/signing locally rather than fighting it entirely in CI for the first pass.\n\n## 5. Known pain points for this app class [verified-current]\n\n- **WebView inconsistencies**: WKWebView (macOS/iOS), WebView2/Chromium (Windows), WebKitGTK (Linux) diverge mainly on cutting-edge CSS (scroll-driven animations, newer grid/container-query features) and some newer JS APIs — standard forms/lists/modals/charts render equivalently. Linux is the messiest: WebKitGTK version is tied to distro/OS updates, so users on older distros can be stuck on old WebKit with real feature gaps. Playwright's own WebKit engine is *not* a perfect stand-in for either WKWebView or WebKitGTK, so don't treat Playwright-WebKit runs as proof of real-webview correctness. Mitigation consensus: test on all three from day one, not after the fact.\n- **IPC payload size for large JSONL logs** — directly relevant to your app: Tauri IPC serializes as JSON by default and this is a well-documented bottleneck (e.g., ~200ms to move 3MB). For payloads \u003e10KB, Tauri already switches to `JSON.parse('{...}')` instead of inline literals as a partial mitigation, but the real fix for a log-viewer is architectural: **don't ship whole logs over IPC** — use pagination/streaming reads from the Go daemon, Tauri v2's `Channel` API for ordered push-streaming, or `ipc::Response`/raw byte transfer instead of JSON+base64 for bulk data. A third-party binary-framing crate (`tauri-wire`, claims 28–33x faster than JSON) exists if this becomes a real bottleneck, but standard advice is pagination first.\n- **Virtualized rendering of huge logs**: TanStack Virtual (headless, 10–15KB, framework-agnostic across React/Vue/Svelte/Solid) is the standard answer — keeps DOM to ~60 rendered elements regardless of list size, has a dedicated pattern for reverse/append-only \"chat/log\" feeds (i.e., tailing), and supports dynamic per-row height measurement for variable-length JSON lines. No Tauri-specific guidance exists, but it's a natural fit for any Vite-frontend Tauri app — combine with IPC pagination/streaming above rather than loading the full JSONL into a JS array first.\n\n## Notes on confidence\nEverything above was checked against Tauri's live v2 docs, GitHub issues, and multiple independent 2026 blog/aggregator sources — not answered from pretraining. The softest points (marked above) are: (a) TanStack pairing with Tauri specifically (no Tauri-authored source, standard web-dev inference), (b) exact bundle-size/startup multipliers (vendor-blog benchmark aggregation, not Tauri's own numbers), (c) \"frontend stack consensus\" is a synthesis across several 2026 comparison articles, not one canonical source.\n","recipient":"team-lead","summary":"Tauri 2.x testing/toolchain research report","to":"team-lead","type":"message"},"tool_input_hash":"3e432551b64dd68e14ca1412fae63728f038e5ddae48d9ab85953294065703f9","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"# Tauri 2.x Ecosystem \\u0026 Testing Research (Sept 20…\",\"message\":\"# Tauri 2.x Ecosystem \\u0026 Testing Research (Sept 2026)\\n\\n## 1. Version \\u0026 capabilities relevant to a dev tool ..."}
