{"ts":"2026-09-09T06:48:18.185408634Z","session":"00f1ab56-8e0a-4c22-8f2c-ed8da8891ca9","tool":"Write","tool_input":{"content":"# Ethos Desktop — Exploration and Build Plan\n\n**Stage:** exploration (pre-spike, no code)\n**Date:** 2026-09-09\n**Author:** claude (COO), from three research streams: Block reference\narchitecture, Tauri 2.x ecosystem/testing (verified current as of Sept 2026),\nand a survey of this repo's GUI-ready backend surfaces.\n**Decision status:** awaiting operator ratification of the decisions in § 10.\n\nThis document is the plan for a native desktop client for ethos, phased by\nincreasing confidence. Each phase spends money only after the previous phase\nhas retired the risks it was designed to retire. No code exists yet; this\ndirectory (`desktop/`) is the future home of the app and this file is its\nfounding document.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Thesis\n\nEthos today is real to *agents* — CLI, MCP, filesystem — and invisible to\n*humans*. The CLI proves the system works; the GUI makes you feel that you\nhave a team. A GUI is what makes ethos real to the user.\n\nTwo hero moments define the product:\n\n1. **Open the app → see your org.** Identity cards, the `reports_to` graph as\n   an org chart, live presence (who is `iam`'d in which repo right now),\n   missions in flight per teammate. A remote-team dashboard: Slack sidebar +\n   org chart + sprint board, fused.\n2. **Point at a line of code → get the story.** `git blame` says *who*;\n   **ethos blame** says *who, under which mission, against which criteria,\n   evaluated by whom, passing in which round*. No other tool in the industry\n   can render this, because no other tool has the sealed audit chunk traveling\n   in the same commit as the work. The audit-seal design is purpose-built for\n   this feature, whether or not we knew it at the time.\n\n### Non-goals\n\n- Not a general git client. Git is the storage layer, not the product.\n- Not an agent chat app. Block's Berd owns \"talk to your agent\"; ethos desktop\n  owns \"see your team and hold its work accountable.\"\n- Not cloud, not multi-user server, not telemetry — the PR/FAQ's local-only\n  commitments hold (prfaq.tex, FAQ \"What happens to my data?\").\n\n---\n\n## 2. The governing invariant\n\n\u003e **GUI features are CLI features with pixels.**\n\nThe desktop app adds zero new state semantics. Every query the GUI needs\nships as a Go-side surface first (a CLI command or a JSON endpoint reusing the\nsame internal packages), and the desktop app fronts it. Consequences:\n\n- No Go logic is ever reimplemented in Rust or TypeScript — three-layer\n  resolution, redaction, seal classification, and the permissive-audit /\n  strict-contract decode asymmetry stay in one place.\n- Everything the GUI shows is scriptable and testable without a webview.\n- The CLI and GUI cannot drift onto separate integration paths — the exact\n  trap Block's goose team documented in their Electron-era postmortem\n  (\"every new feature had to be wired up in both places\").\n\nThis is the single decision that makes \"huge focus on automated testing\"\ntractable: the webview boundary stays thin, so ~90% of the app is testable\nbelow it.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Reference architecture: Berd (and a note on buzz)\n\nThe operator's brief named Block's **buzz** as the model. Research found that\n`block/buzz` (2026) is a self-hostable, Nostr-based team/agent collaboration\nworkspace — signed per-actor keypairs, content-addressed git storage — not a\ndesktop app. Block's actual Tauri desktop app is **Berd** (`block/berd`,\nApache 2.0, launched 2026-08-18): Tauri 2 + React 19, bundling `goose serve`\nas a sidecar child process and speaking to it over a single websocket protocol\n(ACP). This plan models the app on **Berd's construction**, with buzz kept as\na conceptual sidebar (its signed agent identity resonates with ethos's\nidentity thesis and could inform a future ethos feature, not this app).\n\nPatterns adopted from Berd/goose:\n\n| Pattern | Application to ethos |\n|---|---|\n| Backend owns the loop; shell is thin and replaceable | All mission/identity/audit logic stays in the Go binary |\n| Sidecar child process, one transport | `ethos` binary bundled via Tauri's shell plugin `externalBin`; single JSON transport (§ 5) |\n| One protocol, many clients | CLI, MCP, and GUI all read through the same Go packages; no bespoke second path |\n| Local-first storage | Already true: YAML/JSONL in-repo; nothing changes |\n| OS keychain for anything credential-shaped | Ethos holds no secrets today; if the GUI ever does, keychain, never `.punt-labs/` |\n| Ship the updater plugin from day one | `tauri-plugin-updater` wired in the walking skeleton, not retrofitted |\n\nPatterns explicitly avoided:\n\n- **goosed v1's REST+SSE bespoke server followed by a second transport.** We\n  pick one transport in spike S2 and keep it.\n- **Electron.** Goose's own migration measured ~150 MB bundle savings and\n  large idle-memory reduction moving to Tauri; there is no familiarity\n  argument that outweighs it for a from-scratch app.\n- **Business logic in the shell.** Berd's layering is the right one; ours is\n  stricter (the invariant in § 2).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Product and UX\n\n### 4.1 Personas\n\n- **The operator** (human CEO): reviews missions, reads audit trails, wants\n  to trust-but-verify the team. Primary persona; every screen is designed for\n  them first.\n- **The onlooker**: a teammate or manager shown the app in a demo. The Team\n  and Blame screens must land in under ten seconds of looking.\n\n### 4.2 Information architecture — five sections, one window\n\n| Section | Shows | Backed by (exists today) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Team** | Org chart from `reports_to`, identity cards (personality, talents, voice/email/GitHub bindings), live session presence | identities/teams/roles YAML; session roster; `--json` on identity/team/role commands |\n| **Missions** | Board keyed to the mission state machine; mission detail: contract, per-round results with verdict + confidence, event timeline, corrections; pipeline lanes | `mission.Contract`, `Result`, events JSONL, `ShowPayload`/`LogPayload`/`ListEntry`, `ethos find missions` |\n| **Audit** | Per-session tool-call timeline; sealed-vs-live boundary made visible; filter by delegation/tool | Sealed chunks + live tail union; `hook.AuditView`; `QueryAuditByDelegation` |\n| **Blame** | File tree + code view with a mission gutter; line → provenance panel; reverse view: a mission's commit footprint | `browse.go` blame join (to be extracted to CLI, § 6.3) |\n| **Health** | Repo switcher, doctor as a status panel, enablement state, config view | `ethos doctor`, `ethos.yaml` |\n\nFindings the backend survey says are computed but never rendered — all five\nbecome GUI features nearly for free:\n\n- Mission dependency graphs (`TopoSortContracts`) → board lanes and arrows.\n- Write-set conflicts and staleness (`Conflict`, `StalenessInfo`) → live\n  warnings on the board.\n- Evaluator-hash breakdowns (`EvaluatorHashBreakdown`) → \"why was this spawn\n  refused\" explainers.\n- Pipelines (`Contract.Pipeline`, 8 templates) → multi-stage lane view.\n- Full per-session audit timelines (built internally by `readSessionAudit`,\n  then discarded) → the Audit screen.\n\n### 4.3 The Blame screen — three attribution states\n\nAttribution honesty is a product requirement, not a rendering detail. The\nsurvey measured the current chain: authoritative trailer-backed attribution\ncovers ~8.5% of recent commits (17 of the last 200); a loose ticket-heuristic\nfallback covers more; the rest are unattributed. The UI models exactly three\nstates and never blurs them:\n\n1. **Authoritative** — `Mission:`/`Delegation:` commit trailers resolved\n   through `record.yaml` to the delegation's audit trail. Rendered with full\n   confidence: agent, contract, criteria, verdict.\n2. **Heuristic** — ticket-ID parsed from the commit subject, joined via\n   `missions.jsonl`. Rendered visibly softer, labeled as inferred.\n3. **Unattributed** — plain git blame. Rendered honestly as such.\n\nRaising the authoritative fraction is backend work (§ 6.3) and the subject of\nspike S1's kill criterion.\n\n### 4.4 Design language\n\nThis is a *trust* product; the design language is ledger-like. Calm,\ntypographic, generous whitespace, monospace accents for IDs and hashes,\nrestrained color (verdict green/red and seal-state amber earn color; nothing\nelse does). Dark and light themes from day one (system webviews differ; theme\ntokens are cheap early and expensive late).\n\nInteraction principles:\n\n- **Zero spinners.** All data is local; visible loading is a bug, not a\n  state. Anything slower than one frame gets skeletons only in the walking\n  skeleton phase, then gets fixed.\n- **Keyboard-first.** Cmd-K palette from the first screen; every list\n  navigable without a pointer.\n- **Instant search.** Missions, identities, audit lines — all local, all\n  indexed by the daemon, all filter-as-you-type.\n- **Fluid, not animated.** Motion only where it explains causality (a mission\n  advancing a round, a seal landing). 150 ms, ease-out, nothing decorative.\n- **Big logs never cross IPC whole.** Pagination/streaming from the daemon +\n  virtualized rendering (TanStack Virtual) is the architecture for every\n  JSONL view, decided now, not discovered later (§ 8.4).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Architecture\n\n### 5.1 Process model\n\n```text\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Tauri shell (Rust, thin)                   │\n│  - window/menu/tray, updater, deep links   │\n│  - spawns + supervises sidecar             │\n│  - forwards localhost token to frontend    │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ WebView (React + TS)                       │\n│  - five screens, TanStack Query cache      │\n│  - talks HTTP/SSE to the daemon directly   │\n└──────────────┬─────────────────────────────┘\n               │ localhost HTTP + SSE, token-authed\n┌──────────────▼─────────────────────────────┐\n│ ethos serve  (Go sidecar daemon)           │\n│  - same internal packages as the CLI       │\n│  - JSON read API + SSE push                │\n│  - fs-watch on .punt-labs/{ethos,local}    │\n│  - one process, N registered repos         │\n└──────────────┬─────────────────────────────┘\n               │ plain file reads + git\n        .punt-labs/ethos/ (tracked)  .punt-labs/local/ (live)\n```\n\n**Proposed transport: localhost HTTP + JSON with SSE push, token-authed.**\nRationale: the GUI needs *push* (mission events, audit tail, seal landings —\nthe daemon owns fs-watching so the webview never polls), needs *pagination*\n(audit JSONL is unbounded; Tauri IPC serializes JSON and measurably degrades\npast a few MB), and benefits from being *webview-bypassable* (the frontend\ncan call the daemon directly over HTTP, keeping Rust out of the data path\nentirely — the Rust shell then contains almost no code worth testing, which\nis exactly where we want the testing burden: near zero).\n\nThe existing `ethos serve` speaks MCP over stdio; the existing `ethos ui`\nserves HTML with no auth. The daemon here is a third mode that reuses the same\ndata layer: `ethos serve --http` (final flag naming is mdm's call). MCP stdio\nremains the agent surface; HTTP+SSE becomes the GUI surface; both are thin\nfacades over identical Go packages, so the \"one protocol\" lesson is honored\nwhere it matters — logic, not framing. Spike S2 validates this choice against\nthe alternative (per-request CLI exec, no daemon) with measured latency and a\nworking watch design before any UI code depends on it.\n\nSecurity posture (djb review required before P3):\n\n- Bind `127.0.0.1` only; random port; per-launch bearer token handed to the\n  webview by the shell. The current `ethos ui` (no auth, no token) is not an\n  acceptable baseline for a long-lived daemon.\n- Read-only API for P1–P4. Write endpoints arrive only in P5, individually\n  reviewed.\n- Tauri capabilities deny-by-default; one capability file per concern; the\n  main window gets `shell-sidecar`, `updater`, and nothing it doesn't use.\n\n### 5.2 Git: local first, remote later\n\n- **Local (P1–P5):** the daemon reads registered repos' working trees and\n  watches for changes. Blame requires local git anyway. Repo discovery =\n  explicit registration + a scan offer, never a silent filesystem crawl.\n- **Remote (post-P5, spike S6):** reading a repo's `.punt-labs/ethos/` via\n  the GitHub API without a clone — a read-only fleet view of mission history\n  across the org. Valuable, deferred: it multiplies auth complexity and\n  freshness questions and blocks nothing above.\n- **History:** mission records are git-tracked, so time-travel (the board as\n  of any commit) is a `git show` away. Noted as a P4+ feature, not scoped.\n\n### 5.3 Backend workstream (Go, in this repo)\n\nThe survey found the backend is close but not ready. Work items, all\nCLI-first per the invariant:\n\n1. **Extract the blame join.** The full chain (git blame → trailers →\n   `record.yaml` → audit query, cached per SHA) lives only inside the\n   `internal/ui` browse handler. It becomes `ethos blame \u003cfile\u003e` with\n   `--json`, and the HTTP facade fronts that.\n2. **Unify the data layer.** `internal/ui`'s dashboard hand-parses\n   `missions.jsonl` (7 of 18 `TraceSummary` fields) and `browse.go` re-parses\n   `record.yaml` with a line scanner to dodge an import cycle that no longer\n   exists. Refactor into one data package consumed by HTML, JSON, CLI, MCP.\n3. **Open missions are invisible.** `missions.jsonl` gains lines only on\n   close (DES-050), so the board must read the union of the trace log and a\n   mission-directory scan. That union logic belongs in the data package, once.\n4. **Raise authoritative blame coverage.** Trailers fire only on\n   `mission claim` origin (deliberate — the jawp defect taught that stamping\n   the leader's commits is worse than under-stamping). Coverage options to\n   evaluate in S1: post-hoc attribution joining audit-line timestamps +\n   file paths against commit windows; encouraging claim-based flows; a\n   repair command that back-fills attribution as heuristic (never as\n   authoritative). Target: the *worker-authored* commit population, not all\n   commits.\n5. **Reverse index.** Mission → commits needs `git log --grep='Mission: \u003cid\u003e'`\n   wrapped and cached; `TraceSummary.files_changed` is worker-reported and\n   must not masquerade as git truth.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Repo layout and toolchain\n\n### 6.1 Monorepo shape\n\nThe desktop app lives in this repo as one self-contained subdirectory —\nratified by the operator (\"consider a monorepo; one subdir is sufficient\nnow\"). The backend workstream (§ 5.3) and the app that consumes it then move\nin lockstep in single PRs, which is the entire argument for a monorepo here.\n\n```text\ndesktop/\n  PLAN.md                  # this document\n  package.json             # pnpm; no workspace until a second package exists\n  src/                     # React + TypeScript frontend\n    app/                   # routes (TanStack Router)\n    features/              # team/, missions/, audit/, blame/, health/\n    components/            # shared UI (shadcn/ui derived)\n    lib/                   # daemon client, sse, types generated from Go\n  src-tauri/\n    src/                   # thin shell: sidecar supervision, tray, updater\n    capabilities/          # one file per concern, deny-by-default\n    tauri.conf.json\n  tests/\n    component/             # Vitest + Testing Library (+ mockIPC where needed)\n    integration/           # Playwright vs vite dev server + real Go daemon\n    e2e/                   # WebdriverIO + tauri-driver smoke (Linux CI)\n  fixtures -\u003e ../tests/e2e/fixtures/ethos-self   # reuse, don't duplicate\n```\n\nBoundary rules:\n\n- Nothing under `desktop/` imports from or reaches into `internal/`; the\n  daemon API is the only contact surface.\n- The Go module is untouched by frontend tooling; `golangci-lint`, `go test`\n  and friends never see `desktop/`.\n- The plugin marketplace install (`git-subdir`, cone mode on `plugin/`)\n  excludes whole directories — `desktop/` adds zero install weight.\n- Types cross the boundary in one direction: generated from Go (JSON schema\n  or typegen from the payload structs) into `src/lib/`, checked in, with a CI\n  gate that regenerates and diffs.\n\n### 6.2 Toolchain\n\n| Layer | Choice | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shell | Tauri 2.x stable (2.10+) | shell plugin `externalBin` for the Go sidecar; fs plugin only if the shell ever needs it (it shouldn't — the daemon owns watching) |\n| Frontend | React 19 + TypeScript strict + Vite | Dominant Tauri pairing; org agent familiarity = velocity; shadcn/ui is React-only |\n| Styling | Tailwind v4 + shadcn/ui (Base UI primitives) | shadcn defaults moved Radix → Base UI in July 2026 |\n| Data | TanStack Query (daemon cache) + Router + Virtual (all JSONL views) | Virtual is the non-negotiable one (§ 8.4) |\n| Package manager | pnpm | bun not installed fleet-wide; pnpm is boring and fine |\n| Rust | stable, `cargo fmt` + clippy `-D warnings` | shell stays under ~1.5 KLOC or we're doing it wrong (Berd's goosed shell: ~1.6 KLOC for 42 IPC handlers; ours has fewer jobs) |\n\n### 6.3 Quality gates\n\n`make desktop-check`, wired into CI as a path-filtered job (does not run on\nGo-only PRs; Go gates do not run on desktop-only PRs; both run when either\nside touches the shared API types):\n\n```text\npnpm lint        # eslint: typescript-eslint strict + react-hooks\npnpm format:chk  # prettier --check\npnpm typecheck   # tsc --noEmit\npnpm test        # vitest (component tier)\ncargo fmt --check \u0026\u0026 cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo test       # shell unit tier\ncargo audit \u0026\u0026 cargo deny check   # RustSec + license/dupe policy\n```\n\nSuppression policy is the org's: none without operator approval; volume never\nchanges the rule.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Testing strategy\n\nThe pyramid is designed around one fact: **the webview boundary is thin and\nthe data path bypasses Rust entirely**, so the heavy tiers run with no\ndesktop runtime at all.\n\n| Tier | What | Runs where | Volume |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T0 | **Go contract tests** — golden-schema tests on every JSON/SSE surface the GUI consumes, in this repo's existing Go test style | every PR, all platforms | heavy |\n| T1 | **Rust shell unit** — sidecar supervision, token handoff, via `tauri::test` mock runtime (explicitly unstable upstream; keep shallow) | every PR, Linux | light — the shell is thin by design |\n| T2 | **Frontend component** — Vitest + Testing Library; daemon client mocked at fetch level; `@tauri-apps/api/mocks` (`mockIPC` + `clearMocks` in `afterEach`) only for the few shell-invoking components | every PR, Linux | heavy |\n| T3 | **Integration** — Playwright (Chromium) against the vite dev server + a **real `ethos serve` daemon** over the committed fixture repo (`tests/e2e/fixtures/ethos-self`) — real data, real API, no Tauri | every PR, Linux | the core tier; most feature acceptance lives here |\n| T4 | **Native E2E smoke** — WebdriverIO + `tauri-driver` under Xvfb; launch, sidecar boots, one screen renders, quit cleanly | every PR Linux; Windows nightly | thin — single-digit scenarios |\n| T5 | **Visual regression** — Playwright screenshots of component states (Ladle/Storybook), diffed | nightly | medium |\n\nPlatform truth, verified current:\n\n- `tauri-driver` still has **no first-party macOS support** (open since 2023).\n  Workarounds exist (WebdriverIO's embedded-WebDriver plugin, OSS; a paid\n  CrabNebula fork). Position: accept the macOS native-E2E gap at P1–P3;\n  evaluate the embedded-driver route in P4 if macOS-specific webview bugs\n  actually materialize. Playwright's WebKit is *not* a valid stand-in for\n  WKWebView; we don't pretend otherwise.\n- macOS CI minutes bill at 10× Linux. macOS lanes run on release tags and\n  nightly, never per-push. Linux is the every-PR platform; that matches where\n  tauri-driver works anyway.\n- Spike S4 exists to prove this entire table green in CI **before any feature\n  code is written**. A pyramid that arrives after the features is decoration.\n\nFixture strategy: the committed `ethos-self` fixture repo is the shared\nsubstrate for T3; grow it deliberately (a mission in every lifecycle state,\na sealed + live audit pair, a trailer-attributed commit, a ticket-heuristic\ncommit, an unattributed commit) so every attribution state and board column\nhas a stable test anchor.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Spikes\n\nOrdered by risk retired per unit effort. Each is a mission with an explicit\nkill criterion; results append to this document.\n\n### S1 — Blame coverage (CLI-only, no GUI)\n\nExtract the browse-handler join into `ethos blame \u003cfile\u003e --json` and run it\nacross this repo's real history. Measure: fraction of *agent-authored* lines\nattributable at each of the three states; precision of the ticket heuristic\n(it accepts any hyphenated token ≥ 6 chars — measure false positives).\nPrototype one post-hoc attribution strategy (audit-line timestamps + paths\nvs. commit windows) and measure its added coverage.\n**Kill criterion:** if authoritative + high-precision-heuristic coverage of\nagent-authored lines can't plausibly reach ~70%, Blame ships later in the\nphase order and the backend attribution work is promoted to its own track.\n*(Worker: bwk; evaluator: rsc.)*\n\n### S2 — Transport\n\nBuild the minimal `ethos serve --http` facade over the existing data layer:\nthree endpoints (mission list, mission detail, audit page) + one SSE stream\nfed by fs-watch. Measure cold latency, watch-to-push latency, behavior under\nrapid seal/append (inotify vs FSEvents debounce). Compare with per-request\nCLI exec for the same three reads.\n**Kill criterion:** none expected to kill; the spike *decides* daemon vs exec\nwith numbers and produces the API sketch T0 tests will pin.\n*(Worker: bwk; evaluator: mdm on the command/flag surface.)*\n\n### S3 — Walking skeleton\n\n`create-tauri-app` shell in `desktop/`, sidecar-spawn the S2 daemon, render\nthe mission list from the fixture repo. Prove: dev loop (vite HMR + cargo)\non Linux **and** macOS dev machines; a signed local macOS build with the Go\nsidecar embedded (Tauri issue #11992 reports notarization failures precisely\nwith `externalBin` sidecars — this spike must hit that wall early if it\nexists for us).\n**Kill criterion:** sidecar notarization proves intractable → fallback\narchitecture is \"daemon installed by the CLI, app connects to it\" (Berd-style\ndecoupled install), a plan change, not a product change.\n*(Worker: adb; evaluator: kth.)*\n\n### S4 — Testing harness before features\n\nThe § 7 table, green in CI, against the walking skeleton: one real test in\nevery tier, path-filtered workflows, cost measured.\n**Kill criterion:** none; exit is \"the pyramid runs and a PR that breaks each\ntier is demonstrably caught by it.\"\n*(Worker: adb; evaluator: rsc.)*\n\n### S5 — UX fidelity prototype\n\nFake-data clickable of the Missions board and the Blame screen (the two hero\nsurfaces) in the real component stack. Design-review missions: edt (visual,\ninformation density), dna (mental model: does the mission state machine read\nwithout a manual?), mdm (does every screen have a CLI equivalent — invariant\naudit).\n**Kill criterion:** if the Blame interaction doesn't land with fake data, it\nwon't land with real data — redesign before P3, not after.\n\n### S6 — Remote repo read (deferred, post-P5)\n\nGitHub-API read of `.punt-labs/ethos/` from an uncloned repo; auth via the\nuser's existing `gh` credential. Explicitly out of the critical path.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Phases by confidence\n\nEach phase has an entry gate (what must be true to start), an exit gate\n(what must be demonstrated to leave), and names the confidence it purchases.\n\n| Phase | Content | Exit gate | Confidence purchased |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **P0** | This document | Operator ratifies § 10 decisions | Direction is right; risks are named |\n| **P1** | Spikes S1–S4 (S1/S2 first, parallel; S3/S4 next) | Spike results reviewed; go/no-go on architecture; API sketch pinned by T0 tests | The architecture works on this machine, in CI, with real data; blame coverage is a number, not a hope |\n| **P2** | UX definition: S5 prototype + design system tokens + review missions (edt/dna/mdm) | Clickable prototype approved by operator | The product is desirable, not just feasible |\n| **P3** | Read-only alpha: Team + Missions screens on the P1 daemon, full pyramid enforced, updater wired, Linux + macOS dev builds | Operator uses it daily for a week over real repos; T0–T4 green; zero spinner violations | The app is *useful* — the trust threshold |\n| **P4** | Audit + Blame screens; three-state attribution; sealed-boundary visualization; reverse mission-footprint view | Blame demo lands on an onlooker in \u003c10 s; S1 coverage target met in practice | The differentiating features are real |\n| **P5** | Writes (config editing, mission actions — each endpoint djb-reviewed), macOS signing/notarization in CI, distribution (updater channel, versioning), docs | Signed, notarized, auto-updating release installed fresh on a clean machine | It's a product, not a build artifact |\n\nRead-only through P4 is deliberate: the app earns trust with zero risk of\ncorrupting state before it ever gets a write path. Org process per phase:\nevery implementation slice is an ethos mission with commit-per-step criteria;\nthis repo's worker/evaluator pairs apply (Go: bwk/rsc; CLI surface: mdm/rop;\ninfra/CI: adb/kth; security: djb; UX review: edt/dna).\n\n---\n\n## 10. Decisions needed from the operator\n\n1. **Berd, not buzz, as the architectural model** — assumed in this document\n   (buzz is a Nostr coordination platform, not a desktop app). Confirm, or\n   redirect if buzz's concepts were the actual intent.\n2. **Subdirectory name** — `desktop/` (this doc's location). Alternatives:\n   `app/`, `clients/desktop/`. Cheap to change now, annoying later.\n3. **React 19 + shadcn/ui stack** — recommended for ecosystem and agent\n   familiarity; Svelte is the credible lighter alternative. Decide before S3.\n4. **Staffing gap** — the org roster has no TypeScript/React specialist\n   identity. Options: add one to the team registry (a frontend counterpart to\n   bwk), or stretch existing handles. Recommend adding one; the P2–P4 volume\n   is real.\n5. **Apple investment timing** — signing/notarization needs a paid Apple\n   Developer account and real CI budget (macOS minutes at 10×). Recommend:\n   dev-signed local builds through P3, paid account + CI signing at P5 entry.\n6. **CI budget stance** — Linux every PR, Windows nightly, macOS on\n   release/nightly only (assumed in § 7). Confirm.\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A — Backend inventory (survey, 2026-09-09)\n\n- `internal/ui`: 4 HTML routes (`/`, `/missions/{id}`,\n  `/delegations/{mid}/{did}`, `/browse/{path}`), html/template, no JSON, no\n  auth, `127.0.0.1` random port; read-only by construction; two roots\n  (worktree vs git common dir) threaded deliberately.\n- Global `--json` on the root command, honored at ~75 call sites; typed\n  payloads: `ShowPayload`, `LogPayload`, `ListEntry`, `TraceSummary`\n  (`--format json|table|paths` on `find missions`), `AuditView` JSONL.\n- MCP: 12 tools sharing the same payload structs as the CLI.\n- Mission dir: `contract.yaml` (strict decode), `results.yaml` (append-only,\n  per-round verdict/confidence/evidence), sealed event chunks\n  (`log-\u003csession\u003e-\u003cfirst19\u003e-\u003clast19\u003e.jsonl`), `delegations/\u003cid\u003e/record.yaml`\n  + verbatim `prompt.md`, optional `artifacts/`.\n- Audit: sealed `audit-\u003cfirst\u003e-\u003clast\u003e.jsonl` per session under\n  `.punt-labs/ethos/sessions/`, live tail under `.punt-labs/local/`\n  (gitignored, flocked); readers take the union; permissive decode forever;\n  paths pre-redacted (`~`, `\u003crepo\u003e`); `redacted:true` marks stripped payloads.\n- Sealing: pre-commit + mission close; structural verification\n  (`audit.Classify`) fails closed (exit 2) on corrupt/near-miss chunks;\n  quarantine is the sanctioned escape.\n- Blame chain: git blame porcelain → `Mission:`/`Delegation:` trailers\n  (written by `commit-msg.sh` via `ethos hook commit-trailers`, claim-origin\n  only) → `record.yaml` → `QueryAuditByDelegation`; per-SHA cache; measured\n  authoritative coverage ~8.5% of last 200 commits; ticket heuristic fallback\n  is loose by design and must render as heuristic.\n- Computed-but-unrendered: `TopoSortContracts`, `Conflict`/`ConflictPath`,\n  `StalenessInfo`, `EvaluatorHashBreakdown`, pipelines.\n\n## Appendix B — Sources\n\n- Block buzz: engineering.block.xyz/blog/buzz; github.com/block/buzz\n- Berd: github.com/block/berd; VentureBeat and Dataconomy coverage (Aug 2026)\n- Goose Electron→Tauri migration: aaif-goose/goose discussion #7332; goose\n  2.0/ACP blog (Apr 2026)\n- Tauri: v2 release notes (2.10.x, Mar 2026); capabilities docs; mocking\n  guide; `tauri::test` docs (marked unstable); WebDriver CI guide;\n  tauri-apps/tauri#7068 (macOS driver gap); #11992 (sidecar notarization);\n  WebdriverIO desktop-testing platform support\n- CI pricing: GitHub Actions repricing (Jan 2026) — macOS $0.062/min, 10×\n  included-minutes multiplier\n","file_path":"\u003crepo\u003e/desktop/PLAN.md"},"tool_input_hash":"d74147517c297c8f84129205aa4957578bfe02bdacb7b709455a614427dc9926","tool_input_preview":"{\"content\":\"# Ethos Desktop — Exploration and Build Plan\\n\\n**Stage:** exploration (pre-spike, no code)\\n**Date:** 2026-09-09\\n**Author:** claude (COO), from three research streams: Block reference\\..."}
